      Under review as a conference paper at ICLR 2027




000
001
      Reading Is Not Writing:
002   Semantic Competition for Probe Directions
003
004   in Medical Vision-Language Models
005
006
          Anonymous authors
007
          Paper under double-blind review
008
009
010
011                                                 Abstract
012
013              A linear probe reads a clinical concept from a vision-language model’s visual
014              representation. Writing that direction back into the same representation tests
015
                 whether it is a handle on the concept: it must move the concept’s own answer
                 more than every other direction constructed the same way. On chest radiographs,
016
                 it almost never is. At the visual block the language model consumes, we define
017
                 three criteria: whether a concept is readable (probe AUROC above matched
018              random-label controls), answerable (the model’s own yes/no answer beats chance),
019              and owned (a fixed-dose write along the probe direction moves the concept’s answer
020              more than the 95th percentile of 119 random directions, a coordinate-permutation
021              sham, and every competing clinical direction, with simultaneous max-𝑇 bounds).
022              Across 21 checkpoints from nine model families (3B to 72B) on NIH ChestX-ray14
023              and CheXpert Plus, clinical concepts are readable in 56% of checkpoint–concept
024              cells, answerable in 62%, and owned in only 12%. In two thirds of the cells, a
025              competing clinical direction moves the concept’s answer more than its own direction
026
                 does. The seed result reproduces on new patients (ownership −0.071, 95% CI
                 [−0.080, −0.062]). On COCO objects, the same procedure owns 88% of cells, with
027
                 three quarters of every write landing on its own question. The write mechanism is
028
                 intact. The deficit is specific to clinical concepts. The handle the reader responds
029              to exists in the same representation: a direction fitted to the model’s own answers
030              is owned where the probe direction is not. On chest radiographs, this answer
031              direction is nearly orthogonal to the probe direction (cosine 0.1 against 0.6 on
032              natural images). Gemma 3 and MedGemma checkpoints of different sizes share
033              one vision tower and therefore have identical probes and write vectors. Yet they
034              own different concepts. What a written direction does is decided by the reader of
035              the representation—the connector and language model—not by the representation.
036              The protocol, acceptance gates, and per-checkpoint leaderboard form a reusable
037
                 benchmark for concept writability in medical vision-language models.
038
039
      1     Introduction
040
041
      Linear probes are the standard tool for asking what a vision-language model (VLM) represents: fit a
042   classifier to a chosen layer’s activations and read out a concept (Alain & Bengio, 2017; Belinkov,
043   2022). In medical imaging, probes often succeed—clinical findings such as pleural effusion or
044   cardiomegaly are linearly decodable from the visual representation of general-purpose and medical
045   VLMs (Zhu et al., 2026; Pedersen et al., 2026). Activation steering promises the converse operation:
046   write the probe direction back into the representation and move the model’s behaviour (Turner et al.,
047   2023; Panickssery et al., 2024). Together, these operations would make a probe direction a handle on
048   the concept: read it to detect, write it to control. This paper asks whether that handle exists.
049   Showing that steering “works” does not answer the question. A write can change an answer for
050   reasons unrelated to the concept’s label. Any perturbation of matched size can push a yes/no answer.
051   A direction fitted for one finding can carry a generic abnormality signal shared with other findings
052   (Pahde et al., 2025). Random and sham directions test the first possibility. Testing the second requires
053   a comparison steering studies rarely make: write every competing clinical direction at the same locus
      and dose in the same patients, then ask whether the concept’s own direction moves the concept’s own


