      Figure 1: Reading, answering, and writing at one consumed visual block. A probe reads a concept
066
      from the pooled tokens of the final visual block the language model consumes; the same normal is
067   written back to the same tokens at a fixed relative dose; the change in the concept’s own answer is
068   compared with the changes produced by the five competing clinical directions, 119 random directions,
069   and a coordinate-permutation sham. The inset shows the seed case: on the Effusion question of
070   Qwen2.5-VL-7B, the Effusion write raises 𝑃(yes) by 0.190 and the Nodule write by 0.262.
071
072
      answer more than any other direction. We call the outcome ownership. A concept with a readable but
      unowned probe direction is one the model can be read for but not written with—reading is not writing.
073
074   We build an evaluation that tests this distinction at scale (Section 3). At the final visual block whose
075   output the language model consumes, we assign each concept three grades: readable if its probe beats
076   matched random-label controls, answerable if the model’s clean yes/no answer beats chance, and
077   owned if a fixed-dose write along its direction beats the 95th percentile of 119 random directions, a
078
      coordinate-permutation sham, and all five competing clinical directions under simultaneous max-𝑇
      bounds. Seven acceptance gates precede block scoring. We evaluate every contrast in one fixed
079
      batch composition. every NIH and COCO block reports dose response, refit stability, a second
080
      locus, label-shift controls, and prompt-template contrasts. We apply the evaluation to 21 checkpoints
081   from nine families (3B–72B; general-purpose and medical) on two chest-radiograph datasets with
082   report-derived labels, NIH ChestX-ray14 and CheXpert Plus, and on COCO objects as a natural-image
083   control (Section 4).
084
      The results show a consistent pattern (Section 5, Table 1, Figure 2). Clinical concepts are read and
085
      answered in most cells but owned in 12%. In two thirds of the cells, a competing clinical direction
086
      moves the concept’s answer more than the concept’s own direction. The seed result reproduces on
087   patients disjoint from every earlier cohort. The same procedure owns 88% of COCO cells, so the
088   deficit is specific to clinical concepts, not to the write. A direction fitted to the model’s own answers
089   is a handle where the probe direction is not. Its cosine with the probe direction is 0.1 on chest
090   radiographs against 0.6 on natural images. Gemma 3 and MedGemma checkpoints of different sizes
091   share one vision tower, so their probes and write vectors are identical across sizes. Yet which concepts
092   they own changes with the language model that reads the write. Ownership is a property of the reader.
093
094   Contributions. (i) A concept-writability evaluation for VLMs that grades readability, answerability,
095   and ownership at the consumed visual block with matched clinical competitors, a random–sham
096   reference family, simultaneous bounds, and registered acceptance gates. (ii) Its first cross-model
097   measurement: 21 checkpoints, nine families, three datasets, 348 scored concept cells, with every
098
      control reported per block. (iii) Three findings that hold across the grid: clinical concepts are read
      and answered far more often than they are owned; the write mechanism is intact on natural objects;
099
      and the reader of the representation, not the representation, decides ownership. The evaluation, its
100
      scorecards, and the write matrices are released as a benchmark; a new model is added with one
101   adapter naming its consumed block.
102
103
104   2   Background and Related Work
105
106   Probing and its controls. Linear classifiers have long been used to track information across network
107   depth (Alain & Bengio, 2017). A probe score reflects both the representation and the learner fitted to
      it. This motivates matched-capacity probes and control tasks (Hewitt & Liang, 2019). The probing


                                                         2
      Under review as a conference paper at ICLR 2027




108
      literature distinguishes information a readout can recover from information the model uses (Belinkov,
109   2022; Ravichander et al., 2021). Causal erasure pursues the complementary test (Ravfogel et al., 2020;
110   Elazar et al., 2021). Our readability grade adopts the control-task idea: it measures selectivity against
111   random-label probes of equal capacity fitted within recurring image types. Depth-wise probing of
112   medical multimodal models has mapped classification information across towers, connectors, and
113   language layers (Zhu et al., 2026). Layer-wise probes of MedCLIP expose acquisition and device
114   shortcuts (Pedersen et al., 2026). Neither intervenes on the decoded clinical direction at the same
115   locus.
116
117   Steering with probe normals. Activation engineering changes intermediate representations at
118   inference time and measures the resulting output changes (Turner et al., 2023; Panickssery et al.,
119
      2024). A probe normal is an appealing intervention because it supplies a label and a signed vector
      from the same representation. Separability-trained concept vectors can absorb distractor signals
120
      and diverge from the concept they are meant to model (Pahde et al., 2025). A classifier’s weight
121
      vector is a filter, not the pattern it detects (Haufe et al., 2014). Mass-mean directions steer truth
122   judgements in language models more effectively than logistic normals (Marks & Tegmark, 2024).
123   Representation-level surgery on VLM image tokens changes downstream answers (Rajaram et al.,
124   2025). Random-direction and sham controls test generic sensitivity and coordinate dependence. They
125   do not test whether an equally constructed direction with a different label would move the same answer
126   more. Ownership adds this comparison and evaluates it in the forward path at the consumed locus,
127   using each token’s norm as the dose scale. It scores difference-of-means, pattern, orthogonalised, and
128   residualised directions alongside the logistic normal (Section 5.6).
129
130   3     Reading, Answering, and Writing at a Consumed Locus
131
132   We ask three questions about a concept 𝑐 in a checkpoint 𝑀 on a dataset with binary labels: whether
133   𝑐 can be read from the visual representation the language model consumes, whether 𝑀 answers the
134   question about 𝑐, and whether writing 𝑐’s own direction moves 𝑐’s answer more than any alternative.
135   Each question has a fixed estimator and decision rule. Nothing is chosen after observing the outcome.
136
137   3.1   Consumed locus and matched read–write positions
138
139   Every VLM in the grid passes an image through a vision tower and hands a specific tensor to a
140
      connector or merger that produces the language model’s inputs. The primary locus is the output of
      the last visual block whose output that consumer reads, restricted to the token positions the consumer
141
      actually uses (CLS or padding rows are excluded where the consumer drops them); the connector
142
      locus is the consumer’s output. Table 2 names the block for every family. For image 𝑖 and consumed
143   token 𝑡 let h𝑖𝑡 ∈ R𝐷 be the block output; mean pooling over the consumed tokens gives the read
144   vector h𝑖 . The write acts on the same tokens,
145
146
                                              h𝑖𝑡′ = h𝑖𝑡 + 𝛼 ∥h𝑖𝑡 ∥ 2 ŵ𝑐 ,                                   (1)
147   so that the dose 𝛼 is a fraction of each token’s own norm and is comparable across families
148   whose activation scales differ by orders of magnitude. Reading and writing therefore use the same
149   representation and the same positions. Everything downstream of the primary locus—the connector
150   and the language model—is the reader of that representation; ownership is a property of the pair
151   (representation, reader), and Section 5.4 separates the two.
152
