      Figure 5: Same write, different reader. Ownership 𝑂 𝑞 per concept on NIH, CheXpert, and COCO
      for Gemma 3 4B/12B/27B and MedGemma 4B/27B, whose probes and write vectors are identical
480
      across sizes within each family; dots mark owned cells and hatching marks ceiling cells (clean answer
481
      beyond 0.99 or 0.01 on more than 90% of rows).
482
483   blocks are more saturated than chest blocks. Restricting every cell to unsaturated rows preserves all
484   29 owned chest cells. Refits change 𝑂 𝑞 by a median of 0.03. Column selectivity measures the share
485   of a direction’s absolute effect that lands on its own question. It is 0.11 on NIH and 0.13 on CheXpert,
      versus 0.67 on COCO. On CheXpert, rows with a known label retain the sign of 𝑂 𝑞 in 108 of 114


                                                                                              9
      Under review as a conference paper at ICLR 2027




486
      cells. Rescoring the write grid with fp32 weights and batch size one changes 𝑊 by at most 0.002.
487   Every verdict remains unchanged in the 1 rescored block (Table 19).
488
489
      Alternative direction constructions. The deficit is not specific to the logistic normal. Four
490
      alternative directions use the same projected, train-scaled features and follow the same write protocol
491
      (Table 15): the difference of class means, the Haufe pattern Σ𝜷 𝑐 (Haufe et al., 2014), the normal
492   orthogonalised against the other five normals, and the normal refitted on features residualised against
493   the other five labels. Across the 48 blocks scored so far (16 per dataset), every construction owns
494   7–18% of chest cells and 42–88% of COCO cells. The difference-of-means and pattern directions
495   have cosine 0.1–0.4 to the logistic normal. They own more NIH cells and fewer COCO cells than
496   the logistic normal. The chest-to-COCO gap holds under each. At least one construction owns 21
497   of 96 cells on NIH and 30 of 96 on CheXpert, against 88 of 96 on COCO. Extending the clinical
498   family to every additional dataset label with enough support retains 5 of the 6 owned cells in the 8
499   scored blocks (Table 17). We weight writes of the logistic directions by the softmax of each token’s
500
      probe score or write only to the top quarter of tokens at four times the weight (twice the Frobenius
      norm of the uniform write). These writes own 0 of 6 chest concepts and 6 of 6 COCO objects in
501
      Qwen2.5-VL-7B and Qwen3-VL-8B (Table 18). Concentrating the write where the probe reads does
502
      not create a clinical handle.
503
504
      The direction the reader responds to. The reader’s handle lies in the same representation. For
505
      each question, we fit the model’s own clean answer margin by ridge regression on the same features
506
      (3,000 training rows). We lift the fitted direction like a probe normal and write it at the same dose
507   against the same reference family (Table 16). This answer direction 𝑎 𝑞 is a handle where the label
508   direction is not. Across the 36 chest cells of the 9 scored blocks (Table 21), 𝑎 𝑞 is owned in 19 and the
509   label direction in 9. Its write beats the strongest logistic competitor in 30. In Qwen2.5-VL-7B, it is
510   owned for 3 of six NIH concepts (Effusion 𝑂 𝑎 = +0.29 with an own write of 0.81) and all 6 CheXpert
511   concepts. In Lingshu 32B, it is owned for 3 NIH and 5 CheXpert concepts. In Gemma 3 12B, it
512   is owned for 2 NIH and 0 CheXpert concepts. Its raw cosine with the probe normal is 0.06–0.07
513   in Qwen2.5-VL-7B and 0.30–0.33 in Lingshu 32B on the chest sets, against 0.54–0.64 on COCO.
514   Whitening by the training covariance gives 0.36–0.43 against 0.81. The answer direction reads the
515
      label worse than the probe but reads the model’s own answer better (Table 20). On natural images, the
      readable and writable directions coincide. On chest radiographs, the model answers along a direction
516
      the probe does not read.
517
518
519   6   Implications
520
521   For steering, model building, and clinical evaluation. A probe direction that reads a clinical
522   concept is not a handle on it. Probe accuracy and answer accuracy, the two quantities most often
523   reported for medical VLMs, do not establish whether a handle exists. Both are high in this grid,
524   yet ownership is 12%. Beating random and sham controls shows that a steering write reaches the
525   answer, not that the label names the direction. At the same locus and dose, for the same patients, a
526   direction fitted for another finding moves the same answer as much or more in two thirds of clinical
527   cells. The matched-competitor test is cheap once competitors’ directions exist. Studies using probe
528
      normals as interventions should report the write matrix 𝑊, compare ownership 𝑂 𝑞 across layers,
      doses, and models, and pair every clinical write with a natural-image control. The same write vector
529
      produces different ownership in different language models. A vision tower that exposes a finding
530
      linearly therefore does not make its direction a handle.5).
531
532
533   7   Conclusion
534
535   Reading a clinical concept from a vision-language model’s visual representation does not make its
536   probe direction a handle. Across 21 checkpoints and two chest-radiograph datasets, concepts are
537   readable in 56% and answerable in 62% of cells but owned in 12%. The same procedure owns 88%
538   of natural-object cells. The direction the model answers along is a handle and is nearly orthogonal to
539   the probe direction on chest radiographs. Checkpoints sharing a vision tower still differ in what they
      own, so the reader of the representation decides ownership.


