1063
       A.9     Per-image examples
1064
1065   Figure 8 shows grades on individual test images. For a chest example, the pool comprises test rows
1066   where the finding is labelled present, the probe reads it (probability above 0.5), and the clean answer
1067   is No. For a natural-image example, the pool comprises rows where the probe reads the label correctly
1068   and the clean answer is No. Rows in each pool are split by whether the concept’s own write or the
1069   strongest competing write raises the answer more. The row shown is nearest the median gap among
1070   rows where either write raises the answer by at least 0.2. Each example’s last line reports how many
1071
       pool rows share its ordering, giving each example its own base rate.
1072
1073   B       Seed study: registered protocol
1074
1075   The two-model study that motivated the evaluation registered the procedures below; Appendix C
1076   reports its results.
1077
1078   Data and split. We use NIH ChestX-ray14 (Wang et al., 2017), whose findings are mined from
1079   radiology reports. A deterministic hash of patient identity assigns rows to disjoint train, validation,
       and test partitions. The registered manifest contains 18,212 training images and 5,343 test images from


