1326
       Semantic ownership is a contrast on a direction–question response surface, not a property of a
       direction in isolation. Let 𝑊𝑞,𝑑 denote the paired mean change in question 𝑞’s yes probability after
1327
       intervening with direction 𝑑 at the locked dose. A large 𝑊𝑞,𝑑 establishes that 𝑑 can write 𝑞’s answer;
1328
       it does not establish that 𝑑 owns that answer. Within a fixed clinical family, the ownership contrast is
1329
1330                                                              𝑂 𝑞 = 𝑊𝑞,𝑞 − max 𝑊𝑞,𝑑 .                                                                       (8)
                                                                                        𝑑≠𝑞
1331
1332   The registered supplement estimates the Effusion row of this surface, so the reported ownership
1333   contrast 𝑂 Effusion is Effusion minus its maximum alternative. The maximum is recomputed inside each
1334   of 5,000 patient-bootstrap draws. Thus 𝑂 Effusion > 0 means Effusion beats every member of the fixed
1335   clinical family, while 𝑂 Effusion < 0 means at least one alternative is stronger. Adding competitors can
1336   only maintain or decrease 𝑂 𝑞 : a positive advantage is conditional on the candidate family, whereas a
1337
       negative counterexample does not require an exhaustive family. This operational ownership criterion
       measures relative advantage, not a necessary condition for a concept to participate in computation.
1338
       Direction specificity requires a positive Effusion effect above random p95 and absolute sham, together
1339
       with a one-sided 95% lower bound for 𝑂 Effusion above zero.
1340
1341
       Causal-ownership matrix. To test whether another clinical normal better matches the answer path-
1342
       way, we prospectively cross six Qwen directions (Effusion, Atelectasis, Pneumothorax, Cardiomegaly,
1343
       Mass, and Nodule) with their six matching questions on 400 new patients. Every question uses the
1344   same 𝛼 = +0.25, 119 random directions, and a sham. For question 𝑞, ownership requires the diagonal
1345   effect 𝑊𝑞,𝑞 to exceed every other clinical direction, every random direction, and the absolute sham
1346   effect. A centered studentized max-𝑇 bootstrap gives simultaneous 95% lower bounds over all 30
1347   clinical contrasts. The shared-alias family contains two statistics per direction: mean excess over
1348   the matching diagonals on its five off-diagonal questions and signed mean effect on those questions.
1349   These give 12 max-𝑇 bounds; the effect must also exceed the random alias maximum and maximum
       absolute sham effect, detailed in Appendix C.


