1429   Answer-encoding crossover. We next register a paired sensitivity study on 200 patients from the
1430   causal-ownership matrix cohort; the other 200 form a patient-disjoint capability-calibration set. This
1431
       is not the earlier Effusion specificity cohort. Six questions cross the original yes/no prompt with
       A-present and B-present prompts, which exchange the finding-to-letter mapping. Each uses both
1432
       𝛼 = ±0.25, six clinical directions, 20 random directions, and a sham. A question enters the primary
1433
       endpoint only if both clean coded mappings have AUROC one-sided 95% lower bounds above 0.5
1434   and at least ten patients per class on calibration. At 𝛼 = +0.25, we compare squared mean effects
1435   in components that retain or reverse raw A-minus-B sign under the mapping exchange, using an
1436   unbiased estimator and 2,000 joint patient-bootstrap draws. Appendix C defines the components
1437   and full control rule. Descriptive clinical competition uses the contrast in Equation 8, replacing
1438   probability change with finding-present logit-margin change within each prompt.
1439
1440   Independent Mass confirmation. We prospectively test Mass on 200 patients absent from all
1441   preceding Qwen intervention cohorts, retaining the crossover’s 200 calibration patients. Two question
1442   wordings (show, is) cross explicit yes/no, A-present, and B-present instructions; the original yes/no
1443   prompt is a seventh anchor. At 𝛼 = +0.25, each cell receives six clinical directions, 119 random
1444   directions, and a sham. The four coded cells share 20 clinical comparisons with simultaneous 95%
1445
       lower bounds. Confirmation requires positive bounds and Mass effects above every random effect and
       absolute sham, either across all four coded cells or both original-wording cells. Appendix C specifies
1446
       calibration and paired descriptive contrasts.
