1469   Qwen Effusion first satisfies the reader test. Its probe reaches AUROC 0.7742 and controlled
1470   selectivity 0.1166 (95% CI [0.0897, 0.1428]). Original-cohort intervention outcomes maximize the
1471   concept-consistent effect over six fixed nonzero doses. Qwen makes a strong write-direction case
1472   in the original 200-image cohort: the maximum concept-consistent change is 0.2343 at 𝛼 = +0.25,
1473   above the random-direction 95th percentile of 0.0963 and the maximum absolute sham effect of
1474
       0.0852.
1475   The response at the locked dose 𝛼 = +0.25 reproduces on the patient-disjoint 400-patient cohort.
1476   Effusion changes mean 𝑃(yes) by 0.1945, again above the random-direction 95th percentile of 0.0878
1477   and absolute sham effect of 0.0157. None of these patients appears in the original intervention
1478   cohort, and the dose was not reselected, supporting a large and reproducible non-generic effect within
1479   ChestX-ray14.
1480
1481   C.2   Semantic competition changes attribution, not efficacy
1482
1483   The fixed clinical family provides a harder identification test than non-semantic controls. Nodule
1484   changes the Effusion answer by 0.2519 and realizes the point-estimate maximum among the five
1485   alternatives. The Effusion ownership contrast 𝑂 Effusion is −0.0573. The registered fixed-family
1486   endpoint recomputes the maximum inside each of 5,000 patient-bootstrap draws, giving a two-sided
1487   95% CI of [−0.0694, −0.0450] and a one-sided 95% lower bound of −0.0678. The entire interval
1488
       is below zero. Descriptively, all five alternative clinical normals move the Effusion endpoint in the
       same positive direction; Effusion exceeds four by 0.1172–0.1778, while Nodule alone has a larger
1489
       point estimate. This shared-sign pattern motivates an answer-sensitive-alias hypothesis but cannot
1490
       distinguish it from question-specific ownership.
1491
1492   Qwen Effusion therefore remains a reproducible direction that changes the answer beyond random and
1493   sham perturbations, while its specific semantic attribution fails. The familywise endpoint establishes
1494
       that at least one fixed clinical alternative is stronger; Nodule is the point-estimate maximizer, not a
       separately familywise-identified semantic owner.
1495
1496
1497   C.3   Wording changes clinical competition within the answer interface
1498
1499   The second finding concerns the conditions of clinical competition. The Qwen ownership pattern
1500   persists in logit coordinates, so we test whether changing the answer interface changes a direction’s
1501   relative advantage.
1502   In the registered crossover, Atelectasis, Pneumothorax, Mass, and Nodule pass independent capability
1503   calibration. On these four questions, mapping-even minus mapping-odd response energy is −2.1786
1504   (95% CI [−2.4061, −1.9810]): effects are larger in the component that reverses raw A-minus-B sign
1505   when the finding-to-letter mapping is exchanged. Yet all 20 random directions and the sham are
1506   also mapping-odd dominant. Aggregate mapping response therefore does not distinguish the clinical
1507   normals from generic perturbations.
1508   The crossover identifies Mass as a prompt-conditioned candidate: its clinical margin changes from
1509   −0.7181 under the original yes/no prompt to 0.2825 and 0.2900 under A-present and B-present,
1510   respectively (Table 24). A-present assigns A to presence and B to absence; B-present reverses
1511   those assignments. Both coded effects exceed the discovery study’s 20 random directions and sham.
       Because wording and instructions also change, we test them separately on independent patients.


